okay thank you very much it was a  passion project spearheaded by two  former alberta politicians with deep  personal ties to ukraine  former premier ed stalmak who is of  ukrainian descent and former deputy  premier thomas lukaszek who came to  canada as a polish refugee i remember  waking up with tanks and cannons and  armored vehicles in front of our  apartment in the academia poland  complements of the soviet union  within weeks of russia's invasion and  with the support of people from across  western canada the group collected and  shipped more than 300 tons of much  needed aid the medical supplies search  and rescue equipment and personal care  items filled two warehouses before it  was sent to the region by both air and  sea most of our items end up somewhere  in poland and then delivered in minivans  and passenger vehicles  to specific locations to hospitals  medical clinics to shelters orphanages  but the journeys to those towns and  cities hardest hit in the eastern part  of ukraine can be perilous and on  saturday lou kazakh was forced to  deliver tragic news a couple days ago we  lost a couple of drivers  one  polish one ukrainian national there were  three one manage thankfully to escape i  can't share  the exact circumstances but they were  intercepted two were killed  paul hughes knows this risk first hand  the former canadian soldier from calgary  has been in ukraine since march  completing 50 aid delivery missions  across the country so far predominantly  medical aid and food  and shelter related supplies some of  these missions are three-day missions  like it's  it's uh the road system here is  very challenging  despite the danger lukaszic says most of  the aid collected in alberta has been  able to get to those in need thanks to  the heroism of these drivers and there's  no other way to describe it  but more than two months into fighting  the calls for help are only growing  louder the kha'zix team is now focusing  on raising money to purchase food and  medical aid while paul hughes is  prepared to keep making deliveries for  up to another year  heather urex west global news calgary
 

